<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fourah Bay College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fourah Bay College is a college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, founded by the Church Missionary Society in 1827, the first Western-style institution of higher learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Affiliated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a> from 1876, it trained clergy, teachers and clerks for much of British West Africa, earning Freetown the name &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of West Africa&quot;. It is now part of the University of Sierra Leone.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourah-bay-college.jpg" alt="Fourah Bay College" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fourah_Bay_College_shield.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The college opened on 18 February 1827 as an Anglican missionary school of the Church Missionary Society, with support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Charles MacCarthy</a>, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. Its first student was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</a>, later Anglican bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It soon drew Sierra Leone Creoles and other Africans, among them Nigerians, Ghanaians and Ivorians, above all in theology and education. Governor William Fergusson laid the foundation stone of its first building in 1845, and the work was supervised by Edward Jones, an African-American missionary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> who became the college's first black principal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The original building stayed in use until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, when the college moved for a time outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>; it then housed the headquarters of the Sierra Leone Government Railway and later a magistrate's court, was proclaimed a National Monument in 1955, fell out of use in 1990 and burned in 1999. The college was affiliated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a> until 1967. Davidson Nicol became its first Sierra Leonean principal in 1966, the year work began on an Institute of African Studies, half of whose £40,000 cost came from the UK Technical Assistance Programme, with Michael Crowder as its first director.</p><p>In 1985 unrest broke out after a purge of those suspected of militancy inspired by Gaddafi's Green Book, and retaliatory violence and arrests followed. By 1998 and 1999 the college had around 2,000 students in four faculties and five institutes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its graduates include Lati Hyde-Forster, its first woman graduate; Samuel Lewis, first mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>; Africanus Horton, surgeon and political thinker; Christian Frederick Cole, the first black graduate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>; Henry Lightfoot Boston, the first African Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>; and Kenneth Dike, later vice-chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fourah Bay College” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Fourah Bay College</b>, founded in 1827, trained clergy and clerks for much of West Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
